---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import ComponentPage from "../../../layouts/ComponentPage.astro";
import AlertDemo from "../../../components/AlertDemo";
import { codeTheme } from "../../../components/code-theme";
import { componentExamples } from "../../../data/snippets";

const { insertExample, liveExample, variantsExample, assertiveExample } = componentExamples.alert;
---

<ComponentPage slug="alert">
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Also known as a callout. It does not interrupt screen readers unless you ask it to.
  </Fragment>

  <AlertDemo slot="demo" client:visible />

  <Fragment slot="usage">
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        <strong>Never color alone.</strong> Each variant has its own icon, and the title says what
        kind of message it is. The icon is <code>aria-hidden</code>, so the title must carry the
        meaning. Pass <code>icon</code> to change it, or <code>icon={"{null}"}</code> for none.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Variants.</strong> <code>default</code> uses the card colors and the ink border;
        <code>destructive</code> colors the border, icon and title, and keeps the description in the
        muted foreground so it stays easy to read.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      By default an alert is static: it has no role, because text that is on the page from the start
      needs no announcement. The <code>live</code> prop turns it into a live region:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        <code>live="polite"</code> gives it <code>role="status"</code>. Screen readers announce
        changes when the user is idle. Use it for most messages.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>live="assertive"</code> gives it <code>role="alert"</code>, which interrupts whatever
        the screen reader is saying. Use it only for important, time-sensitive messages that appear
        or change while the user works, such as a lost connection, never for static content.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="examples">
    <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">A live alert whose text changes</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      Screen readers only announce changes to a live region that already exists. A region inserted
      together with its text is often missed. Keep a live alert mounted and change its text:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={liveExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>

    <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">An alert that appears</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      To show an alert that appears, insert it into a <code>role="status"</code> region that is
      already on the page, as the demo does with "Save draft":
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={insertExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>

    <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">Variants and icons</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      Each variant has its own icon: an <code>i</code> for <code>default</code> and a warning sign
      for <code>destructive</code>. Pass <code>icon</code> to use your own, or <code>null</code> for
      none. The icon is hidden from screen readers, so the title must say what kind of message it
      is.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={variantsExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>

    <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">An urgent error</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      <code>role="alert"</code> interrupts whatever the screen reader is saying, so keep it for
      errors that block the user's work. For an error that appears, insert the alert into an empty
      <code>role="alert"</code> region that is already on the page, so no empty alert shows before
      the error. For an alert that is already on screen and whose text changes, keep it mounted with{" "}
      <code>live="assertive"</code>, which gives it <code>role="alert"</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={assertiveExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <p slot="install-notes" class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    It creates <code>components/ui/alert.tsx</code>, the same path and part names as shadcn's Alert.
    Unlike shadcn's, it has no <code>role="alert"</code> by default; use <code>live</code>. It needs{" "}
    <code>class-variance-authority</code> and your <code>cn()</code> helper; if your
    <code>lib/utils.ts</code> differs from the current shadcn version, the CLI asks before replacing
    it, and answering no is safe.
  </p>
</ComponentPage>
